    "result_text": "I've implemented `answer()` in `app/chat.py`. It covers all four requirements, and the full suite passes (6 tests, 4 of them new for `answer()`).\n\n**What it does**\n- **Retries:** it calls `iter_answer` and joins the text. If the connection fails, or drops partway through the stream, it starts the whole request again, up to `ATTEMPTS` (3) times. Text from a dropped attempt is thrown away, and only the attempt that finishes is returned. If all three fail, it raises `UpstreamUnavailable`.\n- **Timeouts:** these raise `UpstreamTimeout` straight away, with no retry.\n- **Logging:** each failed attempt logs a WARNING on `app.chat` such as `Attempt 2/3 to reach the model provider failed: RemoteProtocolError: peer closed connection without sending complete message body`.\n- **Client:** it uses the client exactly as given.\n\n**Things you should know**\n- **The error message is one layer down.** In the installed openai SDK (3.19.2), both a failed connection and a mid-stream drop come out as `APIConnectionError`, and its message is just \"Connection error.\" The actual network error, with the text ops need, is attached as the error's cause, so that's what gets logged.\n- **All timeouts are treated alike.** The SDK uses the same `APITimeoutError` whether the provider stopped sending data or the connection itself timed out. So a connection timeout also skips the retries and raises `UpstreamTimeout`. If you want connection timeouts retried, `answer()` can't tell the two apart from the error type alone.\n- **The client's own retries still happen.** The SDK retries failed connections by itself before `answer()` sees an error. So one of `answer()`'s attempts can involve several connection tries, as you said, that's the caller's business.\n- **History is copied once.** `answer()` turns `history` into a list at the start, so a one-time iterator isn't used up by the first attempt.\n\n**Tests** (`tests/test_chat.py`) use a fake client and check:\n- a normal answer comes back in one call;\n- a connect"
  },
